LANGSMITH IN THIS PROJECT — EXPLAINER
======================================

1. WHAT LANGSMITH IS
---------------------
LangSmith is LangChain's observability/tracing platform. It automatically
records every step an LLM-based pipeline takes (each node, each prompt,
each model response) as a structured "trace," viewable at langsmith.com,
without writing manual logging code.

2. WHY IT MATTERS FOR THIS PROJECT
-----------------------------------
The agent isn't one LLM call -- it's a 10-node LangGraph plus external tool
calls (New Relic, Chroma) plus a second service (capstone-ui's Pipeline
Agent) orchestrating it. LangSmith gives one clickable tree per incident
showing every node's input/output, every LLM prompt+response, token usage,
and latency.

3. HOW IT'S WIRED IN -- AIDIAGNOSIS SIDE (zero-code auto-tracing)
------------------------------------------------------------------
- app/config.py defines: langsmith_tracing, langsmith_api_key,
  langsmith_project, langsmith_endpoint.
- app/main.py exports them as real environment variables at startup:

    if settings.langsmith_tracing and settings.langsmith_api_key:
        os.environ["LANGSMITH_TRACING"] = "true"
        os.environ["LANGSMITH_API_KEY"] = settings.langsmith_api_key
        os.environ["LANGSMITH_PROJECT"] = settings.langsmith_project
        os.environ["LANGSMITH_ENDPOINT"] = settings.langsmith_endpoint

- Key point: no direct LangSmith SDK calls exist inside nodes.py or
  llm_client.py. Because `langsmith` is a transitive dependency of
  langchain-core/langgraph, setting these env vars alone makes LangChain/
  LangGraph auto-instrument every graph node and every ChatGroq call.

4. HOW IT'S WIRED IN -- CAPSTONE-UI SIDE (manual @traceable)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Outside the LangGraph, auto-tracing doesn't apply, so
app/agents/pipeline_agent.py manually decorates three async functions:

    @traceable(name="pipeline_agent.start", run_type="chain")
    @traceable(name="pipeline_agent.poll_tick", run_type="chain")
    @traceable(name="pipeline_agent.diagnosis_and_remediation", run_type="chain")

This extends the trace tree upward so one incident's trace can show:
Pipeline Agent decision -> call to AiDiagnosis -> AiDiagnosis's 10 LangGraph
nodes -> 2 Groq LLM calls, all as one connected tree across two services.

5. CORRELATION DESIGN -- FILTERING BY ONE INCIDENT
-----------------------------------------------------
app/api/routes.py attaches a run_config to every graph invocation:

    run_config = {
        "run_name": f"ai-diagnosis:{trigger.incident_id}",
        "tags": [f"tenant:{trigger.tenant_id}", f"adapter:{trigger.adapter}"],
        "metadata": {
            "tenant_id": ..., "incident_id": ..., "pipeline": ..., "adapter": ...
        },
    }
    graph.invoke({"trigger": trigger}, config=run_config)

Every trace is tagged with tenant_id, incident_id, adapter, and pipeline --
so on langsmith.com you can filter/search by any of those and instantly
pull up every LLM call for one specific tenant or incident.

6. WHAT YOU'LL SEE ON LANGSMITH.COM
----------------------------------------
- Projects: "capstone-incident" (the langsmith_project setting) -- all
  traces land under this one project.
- Traces list: one row per graph.invoke() call, named
  "ai-diagnosis:{incident_id}", sortable/filterable.
- Run tree: nested tree -- whole graph run -> each of the 10 nodes
  (validate_trigger, fetch_logs, diagnose_failure, ...) as child spans,
  and inside diagnose_failure/generate_remediation, the actual ChatGroq
  call as a grandchild span.
- Inputs/Outputs per span: for diagnose_failure, the full prompt sent to
  Groq (adapter context + similar incidents + logs) and the exact
  structured JSON (Diagnosis) it returned.
- Latency per span: milliseconds per node (New Relic fetch vs. LLM call
  vs. Chroma query) -- good for spotting bottlenecks.
- Token usage / cost: per-LLM-call token counts (prompt + completion),
  aggregated per trace.
- Tags/Metadata panel: tenant:tenant_002, adapter:spark, incident_id --
  click a tag to filter the whole dashboard to just that tenant's history.
- Errors: any node that raises (e.g. validate_incident's ValueError for
  missing error_message) shows as a failed run with the exception + stack
  trace.

7. SUGGESTED LIVE-DEMO SCRIPT
----------------------------------
1. Trigger a real diagnosis (fail a pipeline for tenant_002).
2. Open langsmith.com -> capstone-incident project -> find the trace named
   ai-diagnosis:<incident_id>.
3. Expand the tree -- point to each of the 10 node spans in order, matching
   them to workflow.py's graph shape.
4. Click into the diagnose_failure span -> show the actual prompt text next
   to the structured Diagnosis JSON output -- "this is the evidence it was
   grounded in, not a hallucination."
5. Click into generate_remediation -> show the proposed actions, then point
   out that validate_remediation is a separate, non-LLM span right after it
   -- proving the guardrail step.
6. Filter the whole project by tag:tenant:tenant_002 to show multi-tenant
   trace isolation.

